body:
## Summary

Icarus cannot translate a legal non-random function call used in an inline randomization constraint. The Caliptra SoC-IFC INTRBLK testbench uses a mask function with a non-rand register-name argument. Precomputing the mask before `randomize()` is a working testbench workaround. This is distinct from closed issue #494, which is specifically about queue-size leaves passed to constraint functions.

## Minimal reproducer

```systemverilog
package p;
  typedef bit [31:0] dword_t;
  function automatic dword_t get_mask(input string name);
    return name == "WO" ? 32'h0000_0001 : 32'h0000_0000;
  endfunction
  class Transaction;
    rand dword_t data;
  endclass
endpackage
module top;
  import p::*;
  Transaction wrtrans;
  string rname;
  initial begin
    wrtrans = new();
    rname = "WO";
    wrtrans.randomize() with { wrtrans.data & get_mask(rname) != 0; };
    if ((wrtrans.data & get_mask(rname)) == 0) $fatal(1, "constraint not met");
  end
endmodule
```

Compile with `iverilog -g2017 -gassertions -s top method-in-constraint.sv -o test.vvp`. A control assigning `wr_mask = get_mask(rname)` before `randomize()` and using `wr_mask` inside the constraint compiles and runs on the same compilers.

## Observed

Pinned Icarus 9b5fad2afb4104caa816b257d5bccc40f01b8c66, c339, and current main ca559fd97b19bd6a56cc10f0faf8583b4077975f reject the function-call form with `constraint ... could not be translated and the randomize call cannot be compiled`. A class-method form produces Icarus's explicit `requires staged evaluation and is not supported` diagnostic.

Verilator 5.032 does not pass the equivalent reproducer: the package-function form hits an internal package-scope error, and the class-method variant does not meet its constraint. No cross-simulator pass is claimed.

## Expected

Evaluate a non-random function call with non-random arguments in an inline constraint, as required for function calls in constraints by IEEE 1800-2017 §18.5.12, or emit a precise unsupported-feature diagnostic.

## Evidence

Minimal cases and cross-compiler results are retained under `evidence/caliptra-bfm-m17-20261019/diagnostics/socifc-mask-constraint/`.

KeyVault's generated environment sequence uses a type-parameterized UVMF base class whose `configuration` property has type `CONFIG_T`, then accesses a nested agent's sequencer:

```systemverilog
class uvmf_virtual_sequence_base #(type CONFIG_T);
  CONFIG_T configuration;
endclass

class kv_env_sequence_base #(type CONFIG_T)
  extends uvmf_virtual_sequence_base#(.CONFIG_T(CONFIG_T));
  virtual task body();
    if (configuration.kv_hmac_write_agent_config.sequencer != null)
      configuration.kv_hmac_write_agent_config.sequencer.observed = 1;
  endtask
endclass
```

The full minimized case is in `parameterized_config_path.sv`. Pinned Icarus 13.0 (source `9b5fad2afb4104caa816b257d5bccc40f01b8c66`, `iverilog` SHA-256 `6cd9749cb1e981820f64fdc737ae458bf6c46e3fcad8927afaf92649ee9a3288`) rejects the nested path at elaboration with `sorry: Nested member path not yet supported for class properties.` Verilator 5.032 compiles and runs the same case, printing `PARAMETERIZED_CONFIG_PATH_OK`.

A paired control casts the inherited `CONFIG_T configuration` property to a concrete `kv_env_configuration kv_cfg` and accesses `kv_cfg.kv_hmac_write_agent_config.sequencer`; it compiles and runs on both tools. The previous non-parameterized nested task-call reproducer also passed both tools, so this reduced case isolates the type-parameterized base-property path.

Slurm job 2797 used one CPU with per-command 60-second limits. Icarus primary compile exits 1; Icarus control compile/run exits 0/0; Verilator primary and control compile/run exit 0/0. Raw logs, commands, source hashes and resource records are in `raw/parameterized-job-2797.tar.gz` (SHA-256 `32afa1ab7b20e2c04a9fdfb1242454f368b32e0bacd34fe5f7ae04b7b00ae4ce`). Please classify and address this Icarus support gap for A3.

## Summary

Pinned Icarus 13.0 rejects an automatic-bin coverpoint on a 512-bit class property during elaboration. The otherwise identical 64-bit control compiles and runs. This construct is copied from Caliptra's generated `SHA512_out_transaction_coverage.svh`, where `OUTPUT_TEXT_WIDTH` defaults to 512 and `result` is the coverpoint.

## Reproducer (512-bit)

```systemverilog
class sha512_result_transaction;
  bit [511:0] result;
endclass

class wide_result_coverage;
  sha512_result_transaction coverage_trans;
  covergroup result_cg;
    option.auto_bin_max = 1024;
    result: coverpoint coverage_trans.result;
  endgroup

  function new();
    coverage_trans = new();
    result_cg = new();
  endfunction
endclass

module wide_auto_bins;
  wide_result_coverage coverage;
  initial begin
    coverage = new();
    coverage.result_cg.sample();
    $display("WIDE_AUTO_BINS_OK");
    $finish;
  end
endmodule
```

## Control (64-bit)

```systemverilog
class sha512_result_transaction;
  bit [63:0] result;
endclass

class wide_result_coverage;
  sha512_result_transaction coverage_trans;
  covergroup result_cg;
    option.auto_bin_max = 1024;
    result: coverpoint coverage_trans.result;
  endgroup

  function new();
    coverage_trans = new();
    result_cg = new();
  endfunction
endclass

module narrow_auto_bins_control;
  wide_result_coverage coverage;
  initial begin
    coverage = new();
    coverage.result_cg.sample();
    $display("NARROW_AUTO_BINS_OK");
    $finish;
  end
endmodule
```

## Pinned result

- Icarus: 13.0 devel, source `9b5fad2afb4104caa816b257d5bccc40f01b8c66`; `iverilog` SHA256 `6cd9749cb1e981820f64fdc737ae458bf6c46e3fcad8927afaf92649ee9a3288`.
- 64-bit control: compile and run exit 0; prints `NARROW_AUTO_BINS_OK`.
- 512-bit case: compile exit 1 with `automatic bins for a coverpoint wider than 64 bits are not represented`; simulation does not start.
- The control also warns that this class-property sample is currently treated as constant zero. That separate coverage-sampling limitation is visible in the raw log and is not hidden by this report.

Commands: `iverilog -g2012 -s <top> -o <top>.vvp <source.sv`, then `vvp -n <top>.vvp` for the control. Each command had a 60-second timeout. Both source files, complete logs, tool hashes, and the Slurm run metadata are preserved in `evidence/caliptra-bfm-m16-20261018/diagnostics/icarus-wide-coverpoint-bins/` in the QD-BFM worktree.

## Caliptra impact

Generated SHA-512 row 17 (`SHA512_random_test`) stops before simulation at `SHA512_out_transaction_coverage.svh:50` because `coverage_trans.result` is 512 bits. That row also has a separate no-parentheses string-concatenation error already tracked in internal issue #664. No RTL, firmware, or pass criteria are involved.

## Summary

A minimal UVM object hierarchy derived from MLDSA generated-UVMF row 96 produces a runtime `$cast` discrepancy on pinned Icarus. The factory reports a matching type wrapper, but casts from the MVC base to both the typedef alias and generated parameterized class return false. The row-96 scoreboard cast failed at 425 ns in job 2755 after printing `alias_wrapper_match=1`.

The reduced case has no queue or analysis port. Removing only the class-scope `DATA_WIDTH` localparam makes the typedef-alias cast succeed on Icarus, which isolates the trigger.

## Reproducer

```systemverilog
`include "uvm_macros.svh"

package mvc_pkg;
  import uvm_pkg::*;

  class mvc_sequence_item_base extends uvm_pkg::uvm_sequence_item;
    `uvm_object_utils(mvc_sequence_item_base)

    function new(string name = "mvc_sequence_item_base");
      super.new(name);
    endfunction
  endclass
endpackage

package mgc_ahb_v2_0_pkg;
  import uvm_pkg::*;

  class ahb_master_burst_transfer #(
    parameter int NUM_MASTERS = 1,
    parameter int MASTER_BITS = 1,
    parameter int NUM_SLAVES = 1,
    parameter int ADDRESS_WIDTH = 32,
    parameter int WDATA_WIDTH = 32,
    parameter int RDATA_WIDTH = 32
  ) extends mvc_pkg::mvc_sequence_item_base;
    localparam int DATA_WIDTH = WDATA_WIDTH > RDATA_WIDTH ? WDATA_WIDTH : RDATA_WIDTH;

    `uvm_object_param_utils(ahb_master_burst_transfer #(NUM_MASTERS, MASTER_BITS,
      NUM_SLAVES, ADDRESS_WIDTH, WDATA_WIDTH, RDATA_WIDTH))

    function new(string name = "ahb_master_burst_transfer");
      super.new(name);
    endfunction
  endclass
endpackage

package ahb_lite_caliptra_uvm_pkg;
  typedef mgc_ahb_v2_0_pkg::ahb_master_burst_transfer #(1, 1, 1, 32, 32, 32)
    ahb_lite_caliptra_mvc_transfer;
endpackage

package qvip_ahb_lite_slave_params_pkg;
  class ahb_lite_slave_0_params;
    localparam int AHB_NUM_MASTERS = 1;
    localparam int AHB_NUM_MASTER_BITS = 1;
    localparam int AHB_NUM_SLAVES = 1;
    localparam int AHB_ADDRESS_WIDTH = 32;
    localparam int AHB_WDATA_WIDTH = 32;
    localparam int AHB_RDATA_WIDTH = 32;
  endclass
endpackage

package mldsa_env_pkg;
  typedef mgc_ahb_v2_0_pkg::ahb_master_burst_transfer #(
    qvip_ahb_lite_slave_params_pkg::ahb_lite_slave_0_params::AHB_NUM_MASTERS,
    qvip_ahb_lite_slave_params_pkg::ahb_lite_slave_0_params::AHB_NUM_MASTER_BITS,
    qvip_ahb_lite_slave_params_pkg::ahb_lite_slave_0_params::AHB_NUM_SLAVES,
    qvip_ahb_lite_slave_params_pkg::ahb_lite_slave_0_params::AHB_ADDRESS_WIDTH,
    qvip_ahb_lite_slave_params_pkg::ahb_lite_slave_0_params::AHB_WDATA_WIDTH,
    qvip_ahb_lite_slave_params_pkg::ahb_lite_slave_0_params::AHB_RDATA_WIDTH
  ) generated_transfer_t;
endpackage

module tb;
  mvc_pkg::mvc_sequence_item_base base_item;
  ahb_lite_caliptra_uvm_pkg::ahb_lite_caliptra_mvc_transfer alias_item;
  mldsa_env_pkg::generated_transfer_t generated_item;
  bit alias_cast;
  bit generated_cast;

  initial begin
    generated_item = mldsa_env_pkg::generated_transfer_t::type_id::create("item");
    base_item = generated_item;
    alias_cast = $cast(alias_item, base_item);
    generated_cast = $cast(generated_item, base_item);
    $display("RESULT type=%s alias_wrapper_match=%0d generated_wrapper_match=%0d alias_cast=%0d generated_cast=%0d",
      base_item.get_type_name(),
      base_item.get_object_type() == ahb_lite_caliptra_uvm_pkg::ahb_lite_caliptra_mvc_transfer::type_id::get(),
      base_item.get_object_type() == mldsa_env_pkg::generated_transfer_t::type_id::get(),
      alias_cast, generated_cast);
    $finish;
  end
endmodule
```

## Results

- **Icarus 13.0 devel**, source `9b5fad2afb4104caa816b257d5bccc40f01b8c66`, `iverilog` SHA256 `6cd9749cb1e981820f64fdc737ae458bf6c46e3fcad8927afaf92649ee9a3288`, UVM 2020.3.1: compile and run exit 0, but `alias_wrapper_match=1 generated_wrapper_match=1 alias_cast=0 generated_cast=0`.
- **Control on the same Icarus build**, with only the localparam removed: `alias_wrapper_match=1 generated_wrapper_match=1 alias_cast=1 generated_cast=0`.
- **Verilator 5.051 devel** (`v5.050-196-g7dcd4e0b6`), UVM 2020.3.1: compile and run exit 0; `alias_wrapper_match=1 generated_wrapper_match=1 alias_cast=1 generated_cast=1`. Built with `-DUVM_NO_DPI`; this checks SV object identity and casting, not UVM DPI helpers.

Icarus commands:

```sh
iverilog -uvm -g2017 -s tb -o repro.vvp ahb_uvm_factory_cast.sv
vvp -n repro.vvp
```

Verilator command:

```sh
verilator --binary --timing -j 3 -Wno-fatal -DUVM_NO_DPI \
  --top-module tb --Mdir obj_dir -I/usr/local/lib/ivl/uvm/src \
  /usr/local/lib/ivl/uvm/src/uvm_pkg.sv ahb_uvm_factory_cast.sv
obj_dir/Vtb
```

This is distinct from #663, which concerns compile-time inherited type-parameter member loss. Exact-source logs, resource reports, toolchain hashes, and the local one-line control are in the A2 evidence tree under `evidence/caliptra-bfm-m16-20261018/diagnostics/ahb-uvm-factory-cast/`.

## Summary

Pinned Icarus 13.0 rejects an omitted argument list on a no-argument class function method when the call is used as a string-concatenation operand. The same source compiles and runs on Verilator 5.051 devel. IEEE 1800-2017 Annex A.9.3 note 37 permits omitting parentheses for class methods except for the stated directly-recursive non-void case; this reproducer is not recursive: https://fpga.mit.edu/6205/_static/F25/documentation/1800-2017.pdf

## Reproducer

```systemverilog
module noarg_method_string_concat;
  class child_config;
    function string convert2string();
      return "child";
    endfunction
  endclass

  class env_config;
    child_config child;
    function string convert2string();
      return {"prefix:", child.convert2string};
    endfunction
  endclass

  initial begin
    env_config cfg = new();
    cfg.child = new();
    if (cfg.convert2string() != "prefix:child") $fatal(1, "unexpected string result");
    $display("NOARG_METHOD_STRING_CONCAT_OK");
    $finish;
  end
endmodule
```

Pinned Icarus command: `iverilog -g2017 -s noarg_method_string_concat -o repro.vvp noarg_method_string_concat.sv`

Actual: `error: String concatenation operand 2 must be a string literal or an expression of string type: child.convert2string` (exit 1).

Expected: compile and run, printing `NOARG_METHOD_STRING_CONCAT_OK`.

Controls: the same pinned compiler passes and runs when the call is written `child.convert2string()`; Verilator passes the original unparenthesized form. Toolchain pins, compiler hashes, raw outputs, and source/control files are preserved in `evidence/caliptra-bfm-m16-20261018/diagnostics/icarus-noarg-method-string-concat/`.

## Caliptra impact

Generated MLDSA row 96 has the equivalent expression in `mldsa_env_configuration.svh:92`. It stops that row's compile before simulation when using the clean-room AHB QD-BFM compatibility filelist. No RTL, firmware, or pass criteria are involved.

## Summary

The Caliptra Adams Bridge `butterfly_top_tb` simulation aborts inside VVP after completing its NTT task. I reduced it to a forked task with loop-local variables and repeated `@(posedge clk)` waits. It compiles, then VVP aborts in `vthread_add_event_wait()` at `assert(head)`.

This is not a current-main-only regression: the reduced case reproduces on both Icarus c339 and current main. The failure is still a simulator crash and should not be classified as a passing or ordinary test failure.

## Minimal reproducer

```systemverilog
module tb;
  logic clk = 1'b0;
  integer a;
  integer test_cnt;
  always #5 clk = ~clk;

  task run_test;
    fork
      begin
        for (int j = 0; j < 64; j++) begin
          int k1 = 3;
          int k2 = 2;
          int k3 = 1;
          a = k1 + k2 + k3;
          @(posedge clk);
        end
      end
      begin
        for (int j = 0; j < 64+11; j++) begin
          if (j >= 11 && test_cnt < 256) begin
            test_cnt++;
            a = j - 11;
          end
          @(posedge clk);
        end
      end
    join
    @(posedge clk);
  endtask

  initial begin
    test_cnt = 0;
    run_test();
    $display("MRE_PASS");
    $finish;
  end
endmodule
```

## Results

- Icarus c339, source `c339b9f2287a743aeb7ab6de6528e8d34a4dd602`: compile exits 0; VVP exits 134 with `vthread.cc:10734: vthread_add_event_wait(...): Assertion `head' failed`.
- Current main, source `3335fe7ec1495956c8adc5788e9a53b0f1756ecf`: compile exits 0; VVP exits 134 with the same assertion at `vthread.cc:10762`.
- Control: changing only `task run_test` to `task automatic run_test` passes on both versions.

The reduced source and raw logs are also retained in the QD-BFM evidence tree at `evidence/caliptra-bfm-m7-rebaseline-20261014/repro-butterfly-vvp-null-head/loop-probe/`.

## Caliptra context

The original test is in Adams Bridge `butterfly_top_tb.sv` at source commit `b77e3d899e828d626cfc2a0d26a6b5704cc121e0`, with Caliptra RTL commit `49370266d12cb0c4a8f71b3a0ff7e54ba7d4866e`. The crash occurs in `intt_butterfly2x2_div2_test`, called after `butterfly2x2_test` has printed `NTT operation success`. Missing input-vector warnings in the full test are separate; the standalone reproducer has no external inputs.

The current source comments in `net_event.cc` describe a related null wait-list failure involving merged events across static and automatic scopes. I have not established the exact invalid pointer path beyond the assertion and the passing automatic-task control.

## Summary

Pinned Icarus c339 rejects the clock generator extracted from Caliptra's `caliptra_top_tb.sv`: a `bit core_clk` driven by `core_clk = #5ns ~core_clk;` is diagnosed as an `always` process with no delay. The same extracted clock code builds and runs under Verilator 5.032.

The earlier claim that changing the clock variable from `bit` to `logic` makes it pass on c339 is not reproduced. Job 747 found both the `bit` and initialized-`logic` variants fail without a timescale and pass with one. This issue tracks the c339 rejection of the actual Caliptra construct; the bit-versus-logic distinction remains unconfirmed.

## Minimal reproducer

```systemverilog
module actual_caliptra_clock_generator;
  bit core_clk;

  always begin : clk_gen
    core_clk = #5ns ~core_clk;
  end

  initial begin
    #20ns;
    #1ps;
    if (core_clk !== 1'b0) $fatal(1, "clock did not return low at 20 ns");
    $display("PASS: Caliptra clock generator completed four transitions");
    $finish;
  end
endmodule
```

This preserves the clock declaration and delayed blocking assignment from Caliptra source commit `49370266d12cb0c4a8f71b3a0ff7e54ba7d4866e`, file `src/integration/tb/caliptra_top_tb.sv` (source SHA256 `c212c32da99e90cd3991da65e653998cac3e945d7479abfd640b9d82f47659f9`). That module has no timescale/timeunit declaration. Its generator is under `` `ifndef VERILATOR``; the reducer omits that guard so both tools process the same clock code.

## Results

- Icarus c339, source `c339b9f2287a743aeb7ab6de6528e8d34a4dd602`, binary SHA256 `683c94313f596bba25aa4e6de37efb4e68bb5dadc37394a91a2a0e6078ac26cb`, version 13.0-devel: `iverilog -g2017 -gassertions -s actual_caliptra_clock_generator actual_caliptra_clock_generator.sv -o build/actual.vvp` exits 1 with `always process does not have any delay` / `A runtime infinite loop will occur`.
- Verilator 5.032, binary SHA256 `672a1ccf3468902f66387049f001b04f254bbcece7d5e816e3861715889bf252`: `verilator --binary --timing --build-jobs 1 --top-module actual_caliptra_clock_generator --Mdir build/verilator actual_caliptra_clock_generator.sv`, then `build/verilator/Vactual_caliptra_clock_generator`, exits 0 and prints the four-transition PASS marker.

Raw source, logs, exit codes, tool versions/hashes, and host provenance are preserved in the QD-BFM evidence directory `evidence/caliptra-bfm-environment-matrix-20261011/reproducers/caliptra-top-clock-generator/`.

## IEEE clauses

IEEE 1800-2017/2023 §§19.6 and 19.11.2 define cross-bin construction and coverage computation. Official edition records: [1800-2017](https://standards.ieee.org/ieee/1800/6700/) and [1800-2023](https://standards.ieee.org/ieee/1800/7743/).

## Validation question and reproduced behavior

A legal automatic cross with 17 × 17 × 17 × 17 = 83,521 combinations is omitted by the clean `origin/main` compiler at an internal 65,536-bin ceiling. The compile exits 0 after printing `sorry: cross 'cx' would generate more than 65536 bins (limit 65536); the cross is dropped.` The same occurs under strict `-g2017` and `-g2023`.

The compiler behavior is reproduced, but this issue does **not** assume the LRM requires unlimited cross resources. Establish whether §§19.6/19.11.2 permit this implementation limit, and what visible result a tool may report when it cannot construct the requested cross. Resolve whether this boundary needs full support or an explicit, failing diagnostic in this project.

Self-authored reducer:

```systemverilog
module t;
  int a, b, c, d;
  covergroup cg;
    ca: coverpoint a { bins vals[17] = {[0:16]}; }
    cb: coverpoint b { bins vals[17] = {[0:16]}; }
    cc: coverpoint c { bins vals[17] = {[0:16]}; }
    cd: coverpoint d { bins vals[17] = {[0:16]}; }
    cx: cross ca, cb, cc, cd;
  endgroup
endmodule
```

## Acceptance criteria

- Verify and document whether the LRM permits an implementation-defined automatic cross-size limit.
- If the 83,521-bin cross is required, preserve its full cross and coverage computation.
- If a ceiling is permitted, make resource refusal explicit and actionable; do not silently drop the cross after a successful compile.
- Add paired strict-edition regressions around the supported limit and the rejected case.

This is separate from #508/#509, which cover cross-bin selection predicates rather than Cartesian product size.

## IEEE clauses

IEEE 1800-2017 §§19.6.1.3–19.6.1.4 define the cross-local `CrossValType`/`CrossQueueType` types and use a `CrossQueueType`-returning function as an explicit cross-bin set expression. IEEE 1800-2023 §19.6.1 has the corresponding cross-bin type and set-expression rules. Official edition records: [1800-2017](https://standards.ieee.org/ieee/1800/6700/) and [1800-2023](https://standards.ieee.org/ieee/1800/7743/).

## Gap and evidence

The clean `origin/main` baseline (`af89cfc50be1084cc86c48865f3f6ba78d4512fa`) rejects a legal cross-local function returning `CrossQueueType` and used to name an explicit cross bin. The same source fails under strict `-g2017` and `-g2023` with syntax errors at the function/bin declarations and “unsupported cross body item was ignored.” This is a language-support gap, not a UVM-specific construct.

Self-authored reproducer:

```systemverilog
module t;
  int a, b;
  covergroup cg;
    ca: coverpoint a { bins vals[] = {[0:1]}; }
    cb: coverpoint b { bins vals[] = {[0:1]}; }
    cx: cross ca, cb {
      function CrossQueueType choose_one();
        choose_one.push_back(CrossValType'('{0, 0}));
      endfunction
      bins only00 = choose_one();
    }
  endgroup
endmodule
```

Focused check: `iverilog -g2017 -tnull crossqueue.sv` and `iverilog -g2023 -tnull crossqueue.sv` both reject it on the clean baseline. The source is a standalone reducer; no application-specific syntax is involved.

## Acceptance criteria

- Accept a cross-local function returning `CrossQueueType` and a call to it in an explicit cross-bin set expression in both editions.
- Preserve the selected tuple as one named cross bin and count its hit in coverage.
- Diagnose malformed tuple arity/type and invalid set expressions at compile time rather than silently dropping the declaration.
- Add paired strict-edition regressions for the reproducer and malformed neighbors.

No dependency beyond the ordinary cross-bin set-expression implementation.

## Gap

IEEE 1800-2023 adds real-valued coverpoint bins controlled by the `type_option.real_interval` type option. The compiler silently treats this option as unknown and drops a legal range bin. In strict 2017 mode it also accepts and ignores the 2023-only option instead of rejecting it.

## Reproducer

```systemverilog
module t;
  real value;
  covergroup cg;
    type_option.real_interval = 0.01;
    cp: coverpoint value {
      bins b[] = {[0.75:0.85]};
    }
  endgroup
  cg c = new;
  initial begin
    value = 0.755; c.sample();
    value = 0.845; c.sample();
    $display("coverage=%0f", c.get_inst_coverage());
  end
endmodule
```

IEEE 1800-2023 §19.5.1 and Table 19-3 define the real-valued bins and `real_interval`; this range partitions into ten 0.01-wide bins, so sampling values in the first and last intervals should report 20%. The clean `origin/main` baseline `af89cfc50be1084cc86c48865f3f6ba78d4512fa` reports `sorry: unknown covergroup option 'type_option.real_interval' ... is ignored`, drops the range bin as unrepresentable, and reports 50% in both `-g2023` and `-g2017`.

The clause basis is IEEE Std 1800-2023 §19.5.1 and Table 19-3; `real_interval` is not part of the 2017 edition. See the official [2023 standard record](https://standards.ieee.org/ieee/1800/7743/) and [2017 standard record](https://standards.ieee.org/ieee/1800/6700/).

## Acceptance criteria

- In strict 2023 mode, accept real and shortreal coverpoints with legal real-valued bin ranges and partition bins according to the declared positive `type_option.real_interval`.
- Preserve exact real bin boundaries and coverage results, including adjacent intervals and the inclusive final endpoint.
- Diagnose invalid or missing interval settings and reject the 2023-only feature in strict 2017 mode instead of silently ignoring it.
- Add paired tests for values, ranges, multiple ranges, interval boundaries, and ignore/illegal-bin interaction; verify denominators and partial scores, not only compilation.

## Dependencies

Tolerance range expressions in bins are a separate language feature tracked by [#462](https://github.com/dsellerbrock/iverilog-uvm/issues/462); rand-real constraints are tracked by [#463](https://github.com/dsellerbrock/iverilog-uvm/issues/463).

## Gap

A cross-bin `with` predicate is rejected when its contributing coverpoint uses wildcard bins. IEEE 1800-2017/2023 §19.6.1.2 defines selection by evaluating the `with_covergroup_expression` over value tuples in candidate bin tuples; §19.5.4 defines wildcard-bin value matching. The implementation handles ordinary explicit-value bins but currently cannot evaluate this legal wildcard-bin value set.

## Reproducer

```systemverilog
module t;
  bit [1:0] a;
  bit b;
  covergroup cg;
    ca: coverpoint a {
      wildcard bins low  = {2'b0?};
      wildcard bins high = {2'b1?};
    }
    cb: coverpoint b { bins both = {0, 1}; }
    cx: cross ca, cb {
      bins low_a = binsof(ca) with (ca == 0);
    }
  endgroup
  cg c = new;
endmodule
```

The equivalent control with ordinary `bins low={0,1}; bins high={2,3};` compiles in both strict editions on the clean `origin/main` baseline `af89cfc50be1084cc86c48865f3f6ba78d4512fa`. Replacing those with the legal wildcard bins above makes compilation fail in both editions with `cross bin 'low_a' uses a 'with' predicate that cannot be evaluated for its contributing bins`. The registered `ivtest/ivltests/sv_covergroup_cross_recursive_with_unsupported.v` is a smaller wildcard-bin reproducer of the same diagnostic.

## IEEE basis

IEEE Std 1800-2017/2023 §19.5.4 defines wildcard bins and §19.6.1.2 specifies evaluating `with` over value tuples in the selected cross bins. The optional `matches` policy defaults to one satisfying value tuple, so the `low` pattern contributes a selected bin tuple while `high` does not. See the official [2017 standard record](https://standards.ieee.org/ieee/1800/6700/) and [2023 standard record](https://standards.ieee.org/ieee/1800/7743/).

## Acceptance criteria

- Evaluate legal cross-bin `with` predicates against the value tuples represented by wildcard source bins in strict 2017 and 2023 modes.
- Add paired low/high wildcard-pattern cases proving that the default-one policy selects only candidate bins with a satisfying value tuple.
- Verify sampling and coverage denominators for selected and unselected cross bins; preserve existing four-state sampling rules tracked in [#481](https://github.com/dsellerbrock/iverilog-uvm/issues/481).
- Keep unsupported product-size/resource cases explicitly diagnosed.

## Dependencies

None known. Recursive selector composition and explicit `matches` thresholds are separately tracked by DD-042 and [#508](https://github.com/dsellerbrock/iverilog-uvm/issues/508).

## Gap

Cross-bin `select_expression with (...) matches ...` is rejected as a syntax error. IEEE 1800-2017 Annex A Syntax 19-4 permits the optional `matches integer_covergroup_expression` suffix after a `with` selection. IEEE 1800-2017/2023 §19.6.1.2 defines it as a minimum count of satisfying value tuples (a positive integer or `$`); omission defaults to one.

## Reproducer

```systemverilog
module t;
  int a, b;
  covergroup cg;
    ca: coverpoint a { bins vals = {0, 1}; }
    cb: coverpoint b { bins both = {0, 1}; }
    cx: cross ca, cb {
      bins equal = binsof(ca) with ((ca == 0) || (ca == 1)) matches 2;
    }
  endgroup
  cg c = new;
endmodule
```

On the clean `origin/main` baseline `af89cfc50be1084cc86c48865f3f6ba78d4512fa`, the same `binsof(ca) with (...)` form without `matches` compiles in both strict editions; adding `matches 2` produces a syntax error and “unsupported cross body item was ignored” in both. The registered reducer `ivtest/ivltests/sv_covergroup_cross_recursive_with_matches_unsupported.v` shows the same failure.

## IEEE basis

IEEE Std 1800-2017 Annex A Syntax 19-4 and §19.6.1.2, and IEEE Std 1800-2023 Annex A Syntax 19-4 and §19.6.1.2, define the legal suffix and minimum-satisfying-value-tuple policy. See the official [2017 standard record](https://standards.ieee.org/ieee/1800/6700/) and [2023 standard record](https://standards.ieee.org/ieee/1800/7743/).

## Acceptance criteria

- Accept legal positive-integer `matches` thresholds on cross-bin `with` selections in strict 2017 and 2023 modes.
- Implement the minimum-count rule, including a threshold at the satisfying-tuple count, a threshold above it, `$` (all tuples), and the default-one behavior when omitted.
- Add paired tests that verify selected bin sets and resulting sampled coverage, not just compilation.
- Keep unsupported resource-boundary diagnostics explicit and separate from the standard `matches` semantics.

## Dependencies

None known. The ordinary recursive `with` predicate behavior is tracked in DD-042; this issue is the additional threshold policy.

## IEEE requirement

IEEE 1800-2017 and IEEE 1800-2023 §35.5.6 define imported subroutine formal types. Annex H §H.7.4/Table H.1 maps `shortreal` to C `float`; §H.7.8 permits unpacked aggregate arguments composed of the listed basic types. Therefore fixed-size and open unpacked arrays of `shortreal` are legal imported DPI arguments. The official standard pages are [IEEE 1800-2017](https://standards.ieee.org/ieee/1800/6700/) and [IEEE 1800-2023](https://standards.ieee.org/ieee/1800/7743/).

## Gap and evidence

Scalar shortreal DPI arguments work, but imported shortreal arrays are still rejected because the array bridge does not expose C `float` elements. On the current `main` baseline, the retained fixed-size and open-array reducers each fail compilation with a `sorry` diagnostic saying float-array marshaling is unsupported:

```systemverilog
module shortreal_array_import;
  import "DPI-C" function int sum_fixed(input shortreal values[2]);
  import "DPI-C" function int sum_open(input shortreal values[]);
  shortreal fixed_values[2];
  shortreal open_values[];
  initial begin
    open_values = new[2];
    $display("%0d", sum_fixed(fixed_values));
    $display("%0d", sum_open(open_values));
  end
endmodule
```

The focused repository reducers are `tests/negative/m10_dpi_fixed_shortreal_array_argument.sv` and `tests/negative/m10_dpi_open_shortreal_array_argument.sv`. Both were run with the existing current-main install using `iverilog -g2012 -o /dev/null <reducer>` and rejected with the expected diagnostic.

## Acceptance criteria

- Accept legal imported fixed-size and open unpacked `shortreal` array formals in strict 2017 and 2023 modes.
- Marshal each element as C `float` (including the open-array path), preserving values and direction semantics for input, output, and inout arguments.
- Add paired positive DPI tests with a C implementation that checks received values and writes values back; retain diagnostics for genuinely illegal forms.

## Dependencies

Build on the existing scalar shortreal ABI and unpacked-array DPI marshaling. This is separate from fixed-size array exports; exported open arrays remain prohibited by Annex H §H.8.2.

## Gap

A legal packed-bit selection after an unpacked array selection on a struct member is rejected as though the member could accept only one index.

## Reproducer

```systemverilog
typedef struct {
  logic [7:0] flags[2];
} payload_t;
module t;
  payload_t data;
  integer j = 1;
  logic got;
  initial got = data.flags[j][0];
endmodule
```

The strict reducer reports `Got 2 indices, expecting 1 to index struct member`; selecting the whole `data.flags[j]` element is accepted. The paired reducer and control are recorded in the [focused evidence README](https://github.com/dsellerbrock/iverilog-uvm/blob/main/evidence/caliptra-masking-member-index-triage-20260923/README.md) and [DD-048](https://github.com/dsellerbrock/iverilog-uvm/blob/main/docs/conformance/DISCOVERED_DEBT.md#dd-048--caliptra-masking-struct-member-loses-packed-bit-select).

## IEEE basis

IEEE 1800-2017/2023 §§7.4 and 11.5 define unpacked array indexing and packed bit-selects; a selected struct-member element can itself be selected as a packed vector.

## Acceptance

- Elaborate packed bit/part selects after unpacked-array indexing through struct members for reads and lvalues.
- Preserve dimensions, selected element identity, and out-of-range behavior.
- Add paired tests for reads, writes, whole-element controls, and nested member paths.

